I played DAoC for quite a while Friday night.  I created a Firbolg in a Way that I thought could lead to her being a Ranger...  I got to 5th and found the trainer to choose her Path only to find out that I made a mistake somewhere and couldn't be a Ranger after all.  So she is a Druid.  <P>One person told me to only put points into one focus and another told me to spread them out, so I stopped putting in points to wait and do some research.  I noticed that at 5 she had a new option for her training points...  so more confusion.  <P>Graphics are AWESOME.  Spell effects are way cool and very detailed and unique.  I like that the con system is so detailed - 8 colors with +'s and -'s to further detail each of the color cues on difficulty.  Chain casting is cool.  I love not having to find and buy spells.  Plants, trees, NPC's, and buildings are amazing.<P>Lots of differences to adjust to in terms of moving around and communicating with others.  I don't like that you can't adjust the size or shading of the scrolling windows for messages - only 4 lines of text in view at one time and the scrolling buttons dn't work right.  Tabs to select group/guild/universal messages, make me think that I would miss info from some sources by chossing any one.  There are problems with aggro...  Foggy and I were killing beetles while their buddies walked by ignoring us...  then 4 or 5 red beetles slaughtered Foggy, but ignored me as I finished off their brother.  I don't mind the pause while transitioning from melee to spell casting (your character actually sheathes their weapon and appears to concentrate  <IMG SRC="smile.gif" border="0">), but the length of the pause should be logical and consistent.  Sometimes the message said that I must pause for 1 second, sometimes 3 or 4 seconds, and it didn't seem to relate to how long I had been meleeing in that fight.<P>All in all, I enjoyed playing.  It's a nice change from EQ and reminded me of the things that I love about EQ.  It also reminded me of some of EQ's shortcomings and showed me that they can be addressed.  I'm not sure this game has the depth to keep people playing for years, as EQ has already proven it has, but time will tell if that impression is mistaken or not.<P>--------------------<P>Michelle<BR>Eklah Dechoklah, 34th level Hugger of Tunare's Trees<BR>Brinne Strument, 43rd level Chanteuse en Bleu<BR>Mortana 12th level Cleric Healer and Death Dealer<BR>Glyneth 15th level Paladin and Pal o' Mine<BR>Gaiana Just a 'lil Mage
